# CSRF protection

A page on another site can make a visitor's browser send a request to yours:
a hidden form that submits itself is enough. The browser attaches the
visitor's cookies, because it decides which cookies to send by where the
request is going, not by who asked for it. If a cookie is what says the
visitor is signed in, the request arrives looking like theirs. This is
cross-site request forgery, or CSRF.

[CORS](/docs/middleware/cors) does not stop it. An HTML form post needs no
preflight, so the request reaches your handler whatever your CORS policy
says. The attacker cannot read the response, so this is about writes, not
disclosure: changing an email address, placing an order, deleting an
account.

You need this page if your app changes things on behalf of visitors it
recognises by a cookie. You do not need it for a public read-only site, or
for an API whose clients all sign in with a bearer token, since a hostile
page cannot make a browser send one of those.

## A policy

Name the origins your own pages are served from, turn the policy into
middleware, and add it to the app:

```gleam
import howdy
import howdy/csrf

pub fn app() {
  howdy.new()
  |> howdy.middleware(csrf.middleware(csrf.new(["https://example.com"])))
  |> howdy.controller(signup_controller())
}
```

An origin is the scheme, host and port a page was loaded from. Give each in
full, as `scheme://host` with an optional port, with no path and no trailing
slash. Comparison ignores case. An empty list, or anything that is not an
origin, panics when the policy is built, so a typo shows up at startup.

List every origin that serves your pages, including the ones you develop
on:

```gleam
csrf.new(["https://example.com", "http://localhost:8000"])
```

The literal origin `null` cannot be allowed. Sandboxed frames and some
redirects send it, so trusting it would defeat the check. There are no
wildcards either.

## What the middleware does

`GET`, `HEAD` and `OPTIONS` requests pass straight through, so pages load
and CORS preflights are answered as before.

Every other method is a write, and must show where it came from. Browsers
add an `origin` header to cross-site writes, and scripts cannot change it.
The request must carry exactly one `origin` header, matching one of the
policy's origins. Otherwise the client gets `403` with
`{"error":"forbidden"}`, and the handler never runs. The match is exact: a
different scheme, a different port, or a host that merely starts with an
allowed one are all rejected.

Some proxies and privacy tools strip `origin`. When it is missing
altogether, a single `sec-fetch-site: same-origin` header is accepted in its
place. Browsers set that header themselves, and scripts cannot forge it
either. An `origin` that is present always decides, and a request with
neither header is rejected.

There are no tokens to put in your forms and nothing to keep in a session.
The protection is header-based by design. Every current browser sends these
headers, Go's standard library protects against CSRF the same way with
`http.CrossOriginProtection`, and OWASP's Application Security Verification
Standard (4.0, requirement 13.2.3) lists origin header checks among the
accepted protections.

## Clients that are not browsers

Because a request with neither header is rejected, the middleware also turns
away `curl`, native apps and other servers. Where those sign in with a
bearer token, not a cookie, there is nothing for a hostile page to exploit,
so exempt them:

```gleam
import gleam/http/request

csrf.new(["https://example.com"])
|> csrf.exempt(fn(ctx) {
  case request.get_header(ctx.request, "authorization") {
    Ok("Bearer " <> _) -> True
    _ -> False
  }
})
```

The function runs on every write, and a request it accepts skips the check.
Only exempt requests that carry their own proof of who is asking. Exempting
by path or by user agent hands the hole straight back.

## Checking one route

`csrf.check(ctx, origins)` runs the same check and returns a
`service.Result`, for use in a [guard](/docs/controllers/guards) or a single
handler:

```gleam
fn delete_account(ctx: Context) {
  case csrf.check(ctx, ["https://example.com"]) {
    Ok(Nil) -> remove_account(ctx)
    Error(error) -> service.error_response(ctx, error)
  }
}
```

It does not look at the method, so only call it on routes that write. Unlike
`csrf.new` it does not panic on a malformed origin; one simply never
matches.

## Where to add it

Add it to the app, so that a route added later is covered without anyone
having to remember. Like any middleware that rejects, it goes after
[logging](/docs/middleware/logging), so the `403`s are recorded, and after
[CORS](/docs/middleware/cors):

```gleam
howdy.new()
|> howdy.middleware(logger.log)
|> howdy.middleware(cors.middleware(cors_policy))
|> howdy.middleware(csrf.middleware(csrf_policy))
```

If a separate frontend calls your API with cookies, the origins in the CSRF
policy are the same ones the CORS policy allows with credentials.

Two habits complete the defence:

- Never change anything on a `GET` route. Reads are not checked, and a
  cross-site `GET` is as easy as an image tag.
- Keep `SameSite=Lax` on session cookies, which
  [`cookie.defaults`](/docs/controllers/cookies) already sets. It keeps
  cookies off cross-site form posts in the first place, and the two
  defences fail in different ways.

The optional `howdy_auth` package applies the same origin check to its own
routes and to every route behind its guard. An app that puts all of its
writes behind that guard does not need this middleware as well.

## Testing

Requests from `howdy/testing` carry no `origin`, so once the middleware is
on, a test that writes must say where it comes from:

```gleam
import howdy/testing

pub fn same_site_posts_are_allowed_test() {
  let res =
    testing.post_form("/signups", [#("email", "ada@example.com")])
    |> testing.header("origin", "https://example.com")
    |> testing.send(app())

  assert res.status == 303
}

pub fn cross_site_posts_are_rejected_test() {
  let res =
    testing.post_form("/signups", [#("email", "ada@example.com")])
    |> testing.header("origin", "https://evil.example")
    |> testing.send(app())

  assert res.status == 403
  assert testing.error(res) == Ok("forbidden")
}
```

A helper that adds the header keeps the rest of the test suite short.
